multiline.items.pipe
====================

The operands of `|>` spread over lines by their author or for the width of their line.

Not about the operators of one place joined by another, which begin a family of their own.

- `perLine`: every operand on a line of its own
- `fill`: the operands may share lines, one on a line passing too, and one spanning lines stands on lines of its own
- a list of these words, where each one passes and code matching none of them is written like the first
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `keep`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` .[decision-facts]


multiline.operatorPosition.pipe
===============================

Where `|>` stands at a line break.

- `lineStart`: one ending a line opens the next, unless a comment follows it
- `lineEnd`: one opening a line ends the one before, unless a comment precedes it
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `lineStart`, psr12 `lineStart`, nette `lineStart`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` · covers `operator_linebreak` .[decision-facts]


multiline.items.chain
=====================

The links of a chain of method calls and property accesses spread over lines by its author, or for the width of its line where it has two method calls or more.

- `perLine`: every link on a line of its own
- `fill`: the links may share lines, one on a line passing too, and one spanning lines stands on lines of its own
- a list of these words, where each one passes and code matching none of them is written like the first
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `perLine`, psr12 `keep`, nette `perLine`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` .[decision-facts]

When the line is too long, only a chain of at least two method calls is spread; `$this->customer->getAddress()->city` stays whole. `multiline.edges.chain` says where the links before the first broken one go.

```neon
file:
	lineLength: 60
multiline:
	items:
		chain: perLine
	edges:
		chain: apart
```

```php .[before]
$orders = $repository->findPaid()->sortBy('date')->limit(10)->toArray();  // Expected a line break before the object operator, because the line is 72 characters long, more than 60.
```

```php .[after]
$orders = $repository
	->findPaid()
	->sortBy('date')
	->limit(10)
	->toArray();
```


multiline.edges.chain
=====================

Where the links spread over lines begin against the object.

- `apart`: the first link on the line after the object
- `attached`: the links up to the first method call on the line of the object
- a list of these words, where each one passes and code matching none of them is written like the first
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `apart`, psr12 `keep`, nette `attached`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` .[decision-facts]

With `attached`, all links up to the first method call stay on the line of the object:

```neon
file:
	lineLength: 60
multiline:
	items:
		chain: perLine
	edges:
		chain: attached
```

```php .[before]
$orders = $this->repository->findPaid()->sortBy('date')->limit(10);  // Expected a line break before the object operator, because the line is 67 characters long, more than 60.
```

```php .[after]
$orders = $this->repository->findPaid()
	->sortBy('date')
	->limit(10);
```


multiline.items.matchCondition
==============================

The conditions of a `match` arm spread over lines by their author or for the width of their line, `=>` after the last one.

Written as `fill`, the conditions fill the lines evenly from `multiline.fillMinItems.matchCondition` of them, fewer standing one on a line.

- `perLine`: every condition on a line of its own
- `fill`: the conditions may share lines, one on a line passing too, and one spanning lines stands on lines of its own
- a list of these words, where each one passes and code matching none of them is written like the first
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `perLine`, psr12 `perLine`, nette `fill`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` .[decision-facts]

The conditions of an arm go one per line, with the same indentation as the arm, and `=>` with the result follows the last one; an arm with a single condition stays as it is:

```neon
file:
	lineLength: 60
multiline:
	items:
		matchCondition: perLine
```

```php .[before]
$kind = match (true) {
	$node instanceof FunctionNode, $node instanceof MethodNode => 'function',  // Expected a line break before the expression, because the line is 77 characters long, more than 60.
	default => null,
};
```

```php .[after]
$kind = match (true) {
	$node instanceof FunctionNode,
	$node instanceof MethodNode => 'function',
	default => null,
};
```


multiline.fillMinItems.matchCondition
=====================================

From how many conditions an arm written in `fill` fills its lines.

- a number from 2

Default `2` · standards: perCs `2`, psr12 `2`, nette `6`, symfony `2`, laravel `2` .[decision-facts]


multiline.items.matchArm
========================

The arms of a `match` whose braces stand on different lines.

Not about the conditions of one arm, which `multiline.items.matchCondition` spreads.

- `perLine`: every arm on a line of its own
- a list of these words, where each one passes and code matching none of them is written like the first
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `perLine`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` .[decision-facts]


multiline.items.ternary
=======================

A ternary spread over lines by its author, or for the width of its line.

- `perLine`: every operand on a line of its own
- `fill`: the operands may share lines, one on a line passing too, and one spanning lines stands on lines of its own
- a list of these words, where each one passes and code matching none of them is written like the first
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `perLine`, psr12 `keep`, nette `perLine`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` · covers `SlevomatCodingStandard.ControlStructures.RequireMultiLineTernaryOperator` .[decision-facts]

```neon
file:
	lineLength: 60
multiline:
	items:
		ternary: perLine
```

```php .[before]
$label = $order->isPaid() ? $this->formatDate($order->paidAt) : 'unpaid';  // Expected a line break before the `?` operator, because the line is 73 characters long, more than 60.
```

```php .[after]
$label = $order->isPaid()
	? $this->formatDate($order->paidAt)
	: 'unpaid';
```


multiline.operatorPosition.ternary
==================================

Where `?`, `:` and `?:` of a ternary stand at a line break, of a ternary spread over lines too.

- `lineStart`: one ending a line opens the next, unless a comment follows it
- `lineEnd`: one opening a line ends the one before, unless a comment precedes it
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `lineStart`, psr12 `keep`, nette `lineStart`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` · covers `operator_linebreak`, `SlevomatCodingStandard.ControlStructures.DisallowTrailingMultiLineTernaryOperator` .[decision-facts]


multiline.items.argument
========================

The arguments of a call, of `new` or of an attribute and the variables of `isset()` and `unset()` spread over lines by their author, which is one where an argument or the closing parenthesis begins a line, or for the width of their line, each comma on the line of its argument.

A sole argument that is a string, a message, is not spread for its width.

- `perLine`: every argument on a line of its own
- `fill`: the arguments may share lines, one on a line passing too, and one spanning lines stands on lines of its own
- a list of these words, where each one passes and code matching none of them is written like the first
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `perLine`, psr12 `perLine`, nette `perLine`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` · covers `method_argument_space`, `PSR2.Methods.FunctionCallSignature` .[decision-facts]

On a line that is too long, the value `fill` with the edges `apart` puts only the brackets on lines of their own and keeps the arguments together:

```neon
file:
	lineLength: 60
multiline:
	items:
		argument: fill
	edges:
		argument: apart
```

```php .[before]
$invoice = $this->invoices->create($order, $customer, $dueDate);  // Expected a line break before the argument, because the line is 64 characters long, more than 60.
```

```php .[after]
$invoice = $this->invoices->create(
	$order, $customer, $dueDate
);
```


multiline.edges.argument
========================

Where the arguments spread over lines begin against `(` and where `)` stands.

- `apart`: the first argument on the line after `(`, `)` on a line of its own
- `attached`: the first argument on the line of `(`, `)` right after the last one
- `closingApart`: the first argument on the line of `(`, `)` on a line of its own
- a list of these words, where each one passes and code matching none of them is written like the first
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `apart`, psr12 `apart`, nette `apart`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` · covers `method_argument_space`, `PSR2.Methods.FunctionCallSignature` .[decision-facts]


multiline.spreadItem
====================

What a call too wide spreads where an argument could spread in its place; one so spread by its author stays whatever it says.

- `never`: the call spreads its arguments
- `sole`: a sole argument that is a call, `new` or an array spreads in the parentheses, `foo(bar(` and `))` together
- `last`: as `sole`, and so does the last argument after those before it where the line fits up to its bracket, `foo($a, [` and `])` together

Default `never` · standards: perCs `never`, psr12 `never`, nette `sole`, symfony `never`, laravel `never` .[decision-facts]

Only a sole argument is spread this way; a call with more arguments is spread like any other call:

```neon
file:
	lineLength: 60
multiline:
	items:
		argument: perLine
	edges:
		argument: apart
	spreadItem: sole
```

```php .[before]
throw new InvalidOrder(sprintf('Order %d cannot be shipped.', $id));  // Expected a line break before the argument, because the line is 68 characters long, more than 60.
$this->logger->info(sprintf('Order %d shipped.', $id), ['id' => $id]);  // Expected a line break before the argument, because the line is 70 characters long, more than 60.
```

```php .[after]
throw new InvalidOrder(sprintf(
	'Order %d cannot be shipped.',
	$id
));
$this->logger->info(
	sprintf('Order %d shipped.', $id),
	['id' => $id]
);
```


multiline.items.closureUse
==========================

The variables of the `use` of a closure spread over lines by their author or for their width, each comma on the line of its variable.

- `perLine`: every variable on a line of its own
- `fill`: the variables may share lines, one on a line passing too, and one spanning lines stands on lines of its own
- a list of these words, where each one passes and code matching none of them is written like the first
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `keep`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` .[decision-facts]


multiline.edges.closureUse
==========================

Where the variables spread over lines begin against `(` and where `)` stands.

- `apart`: the first variable on the line after `(`, `)` on a line of its own
- `attached`: the first variable on the line of `(`, `)` right after the last one
- `closingApart`: the first variable on the line of `(`, `)` on a line of its own
- a list of these words, where each one passes and code matching none of them is written like the first
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `keep`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` .[decision-facts]


multiline.items.parameter
=========================

The parameters of a function or a method spread over lines by their author or for their width, or with a parameter whose hooks span lines, each comma on the line of its parameter.

- `perLine`: every parameter on a line of its own
- `fill`: the parameters may share lines, one on a line passing too, and one spanning lines stands on lines of its own
- a list of these words, where each one passes and code matching none of them is written like the first
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `perLine`, psr12 `perLine`, nette `perLine`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` · covers `SlevomatCodingStandard.Classes.RequireMultiLineMethodSignature`, `Squiz.Functions.MultiLineFunctionDeclaration` .[decision-facts]

```neon
file:
	lineLength: 60
multiline:
	items:
		parameter: perLine
	edges:
		parameter: apart
```

```php .[before]
class Shipping
{
	public function ship(Order $order, Address $address, bool $express): void  // Expected a line break before the parameter, because the line is 77 characters long, more than 60.
	{
	}
}
```

```php .[after]
class Shipping
{
	public function ship(
		Order $order,
		Address $address,
		bool $express
	): void
	{
	}
}
```


multiline.edges.parameter
=========================

Where the parameters spread over lines begin against `(` and where `)` stands.

- `apart`: the first parameter on the line after `(`, `)` on a line of its own
- `attached`: the first parameter on the line of `(`, `)` right after the last one
- `closingApart`: the first parameter on the line of `(`, `)` on a line of its own
- a list of these words, where each one passes and code matching none of them is written like the first
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `apart`, psr12 `apart`, nette `apart`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` · covers `SlevomatCodingStandard.Classes.RequireMultiLineMethodSignature`, `Squiz.Functions.MultiLineFunctionDeclaration` .[decision-facts]


multiline.spreadAlways
======================

What is spread over lines whatever its width.

- a list of these words:
	- `promotedProperty`: the parameters of a signature declaring a promoted property

Default `[]` · standards: perCs `[]`, psr12 `[]`, nette `[promotedProperty]`, symfony `[]`, laravel `[]` · covers `multiline_promoted_properties`, `SlevomatCodingStandard.Classes.RequireMultiLineMethodSignature` .[decision-facts]

It is spread into the shape given by `multiline.items.parameter` and `multiline.edges.parameter`. With `promotedProperty` in the list, even a short constructor that declares a promoted property is spread:

```neon
multiline:
	items:
		parameter: perLine
	edges:
		parameter: apart
	spreadAlways: [promotedProperty]
```

```php .[before]
class Shipping
{
	public function __construct(private Carrier $carrier)  // Expected a line break before the parameter, because the signature declares a promoted property.
	{
	}
}
```

```php .[after]
class Shipping
{
	public function __construct(
		private Carrier $carrier
	)
	{
	}
}
```


multiline.items.import
======================

The names of a group use spread over lines, by their author or for the width the import would have on one line.

Written as `fill`, the names fill the lines evenly from `multiline.fillMinItems.import` of them, fewer standing one on a line.

- `perLine`: every name on a line of its own
- `fill`: the names may share lines, one on a line passing too, and one spanning lines stands on lines of its own
- a list of these words, where each one passes and code matching none of them is written like the first
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `fill`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` .[decision-facts]


multiline.edges.import
======================

Where the names spread over lines begin against `{` and where `}` stands.

- `apart`: the first name on the line after `{`, `}` on a line of its own
- `attached`: the first name on the line of `{`, `}` right after the last one
- `closingApart`: the first name on the line of `{`, `}` on a line of its own
- a list of these words, where each one passes and code matching none of them is written like the first
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `apart`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` .[decision-facts]


multiline.fillMinItems.import
=============================

From how many names a group use written in `fill` fills its lines.

- a number from 2

Default `2` · standards: perCs `2`, psr12 `2`, nette `2`, symfony `2`, laravel `2` .[decision-facts]


multiline.attributePosition.declaration
=======================================

The attributes of a class, a function, a method, a property, a constant, a case of an enum or a hook of a property whose hooks span lines stand right above it, no blank line between the groups; those of a closure, an anonymous class or a hook in hooks written on one line may share its line.

- `above`: every group on a line of its own above the declaration
- `sameLine`: every group on the line of the declaration
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `above`, psr12 `keep`, nette `above`, symfony `above`, laravel `keep` · covers `SlevomatCodingStandard.Attributes.DisallowMultipleAttributesPerLine` .[decision-facts]


multiline.attributePosition.parameter
=====================================

Where the attributes of a parameter stand.

- `above`: every group on a line of its own above the parameter in a list of parameters spread over lines, on the line of the parameter in a list on one line
- `sameLine`: every group on the line of the parameter, in a list spread over lines too
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `above`, psr12 `keep`, nette `above`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` · covers `SlevomatCodingStandard.Attributes.DisallowMultipleAttributesPerLine` .[decision-facts]


multiline.expressionStart.return
================================

Where an expression spanning several lines after `return` begins.

- `sameLine`: on the line of what comes before
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `sameLine`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` .[decision-facts]


multiline.expressionStart.throw
===============================

Where an expression spanning several lines after `throw` begins.

- `sameLine`: on the line of what comes before
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `sameLine`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` .[decision-facts]


multiline.expressionStart.yield
===============================

Where an expression spanning several lines after `yield` and `yield from` begins.

- `sameLine`: on the line of what comes before
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `sameLine`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` .[decision-facts]


multiline.expressionStart.echo
==============================

Where an expression spanning several lines after `echo` and `print` begins.

- `sameLine`: on the line of what comes before
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `sameLine`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` .[decision-facts]


multiline.items.attribute
=========================

The attributes of one group spread over lines by their author or for their width, each comma on the line of its attribute.

- `perLine`: every attribute on a line of its own
- `fill`: the attributes may share lines, one on a line passing too, and one spanning lines stands on lines of its own
- a list of these words, where each one passes and code matching none of them is written like the first
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `keep`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` .[decision-facts]


multiline.edges.attribute
=========================

Where the attributes spread over lines begin against `#[` and where `]` stands.

- `apart`: the first attribute on the line after `#[`, `]` on a line of its own
- `attached`: the first attribute on the line of `#[`, `]` right after the last one
- `closingApart`: the first attribute on the line of `#[`, `]` on a line of its own
- a list of these words, where each one passes and code matching none of them is written like the first
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `keep`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` .[decision-facts]


multiline.semicolon
===================

Where the semicolon of a statement spanning several lines stands.

- `sameLine`: closing the last line of the statement
- `nextLine`: on a line of its own below a chain of method calls spread over lines, closing the last line of any other statement
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `sameLine`, symfony `keep`, laravel `sameLine` · covers `multiline_whitespace_before_semicolons`, `Squiz.WhiteSpace.SemicolonSpacing` .[decision-facts]

The value `nextLine` suits a project that writes queries as a method chain: with the semicolon on a line of its own, you can add or delete the last call without changing any other line. Other statements spread over several lines keep the semicolon at the end of the last line, even here.

```neon
multiline:
	semicolon: nextLine
```

```php .[before]
$orders = $this->db->table('orders')
	->where('paid', true)
	->orderBy('created');  // Expected a line break before the semicolon.
```

```php .[after]
$orders = $this->db->table('orders')
	->where('paid', true)
	->orderBy('created')
;
```
